    'body'        => <<<'HTML'
<p>A hurricane insurance claim in Texas often means two or even three separate claims: one for wind, one for flood and sometimes one with the Texas Windstorm Insurance Association (TWIA) if you live on the coast. Report each loss quickly, document everything before repairs, and learn the Texas deadlines that control how fast you get paid and what you must do before you sue.</p>
<p>This guide is written for Houston and Gulf Coast homeowners, landlords and investors who want to file correctly the first time.</p>

<h2>Wind vs flood: why one hurricane can mean two claims</h2>
<p>The single biggest source of confusion after a Texas hurricane is the line between wind and water. Insurers treat them as different perils, often under different policies.</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr><th>Damage type</th><th>Usually covered by</th><th>Examples</th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Wind and wind-driven rain</td><td>Homeowners policy, or TWIA on the coast</td><td>Missing shingles, fallen trees, broken windows, rain entering through a storm-damaged roof</td></tr>
<tr><td>Flood (rising water)</td><td>NFIP or private flood policy</td><td>Storm surge, overflowing bayous, water coming in at ground level</td></tr>
<tr><td>Hail</td><td>Homeowners policy, or TWIA on the coast</td><td>Dented roofing, cracked siding, damaged AC units</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>Standard homeowners policies generally exclude flood. If water came in under the door or up through the floor, that is typically a flood claim. If the roof failed and rain poured in from above, that is typically a wind claim. Many homes have both, and adjusters from each insurer will try to separate them, so your photos and timeline matter.</p>
<p>If you are buying in a lower-lying neighborhood, read <a href="/blog/buying-a-house-in-a-flood-zone-houston/">buying a house in a flood zone in Houston</a> before you close.</p>

<h2>TWIA, NFIP and your homeowners policy</h2>
<h3>TWIA for coastal properties</h3>
<p>Many private insurers will not write wind coverage near the coast. TWIA is the state's insurer of last resort for wind and hail in the designated catastrophe area, which TWIA describes as the 14 first-tier coastal counties and parts of Harris County east of Highway 146. Eligible properties generally need a certificate of compliance showing they were built to applicable windstorm codes. Homeowners in that area often carry a private homeowners policy that excludes wind, a TWIA policy for wind and hail, and a separate flood policy.</p>
<p>TWIA's published timeline is different from private insurers. According to TWIA:</p>
<ul>
<li>You must report a claim within one year of the date your property was damaged.</li>
<li>TWIA has 60 days after receiving all requested information to accept or reject the claim, and 10 days to pay after accepting coverage.</li>
<li>To request appraisal on an actual cash value decision, you generally have 60 days from receiving your notice letter.</li>
<li>To dispute a decision in court, you must notify TWIA and file suit no later than two years after you receive the decision.</li>
</ul>
<h3>NFIP flood insurance</h3>
<p>Most flood coverage in Texas is written through the National Flood Insurance Program. NFIP limits for a single-family home are up to $250,000 for the building and $100,000 for contents. New policies usually have a 30-day waiting period, with exceptions such as coverage purchased when closing a loan, which is why buying a policy when a storm is already in the Gulf usually does not help.</p>
<p>The most important NFIP deadline: a signed proof of loss is generally due within 60 days of the date of loss, unless FEMA extends it after a major disaster. Submit it on time even if you disagree with the adjuster's estimate. You can file a supplemental claim for damage found later.</p>

<h3>Notes for landlords and investors</h3>
<p>If you own rental property, check whether your landlord or dwelling policy includes fair rental value or loss of rents coverage, and what proof it requires. Keep copies of leases, rent rolls and move-out notices for any tenant who leaves because the unit is uninhabitable. Flood policies generally do not pay for lost rent or temporary housing, so that gap needs to be planned for before storm season, not after. If you own several properties, file a separate claim for each address and keep each file organized on its own.</p>

<h2>Hurricane and named storm percentage deductibles</h2>
<p>Many Texas policies use a percentage deductible for wind, hail or named storm losses. The percentage is applied to your dwelling coverage limit, not to the amount of damage. Percentage deductibles in Texas commonly range from about 1% to 5% of dwelling coverage.</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr><th>Dwelling coverage</th><th>1% deductible</th><th>2% deductible</th><th>5% deductible</th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>$250,000</td><td>$2,500</td><td>$5,000</td><td>$12,500</td></tr>
<tr><td>$350,000</td><td>$3,500</td><td>$7,000</td><td>$17,500</td></tr>
<tr><td>$500,000</td><td>$5,000</td><td>$10,000</td><td>$25,000</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>Check your declarations page before storm season, and set aside cash to cover that amount. Your flood policy has its own separate deductible. And remember that no contractor may legally offer to waive or absorb your deductible in Texas.</p>

<h2>Texas claim deadlines under the Prompt Payment of Claims Act</h2>
<p>Chapter 542 of the Texas Insurance Code, often called the Prompt Payment of Claims Act, sets deadlines for licensed insurers. TWIA follows its own statutory timeline described above.</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr><th>Step</th><th>General deadline</th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Acknowledge the claim, begin investigating and request information</td><td>15 days after notice of the claim (30 business days for eligible surplus lines insurers)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Accept or reject in writing</td><td>15 business days after receiving all requested items</td></tr>
<tr><td>Extension if more time is needed</td><td>Up to 45 additional days after the insurer notifies you and explains why</td></tr>
<tr><td>Pay an accepted claim</td><td>5 business days after notifying you of acceptance</td></tr>
<tr><td>Weather catastrophe or major natural disaster</td><td>Deadlines extended by 15 additional days</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>If an insurer misses these deadlines, Texas law can require it to pay interest on the late amount plus reasonable attorney fees. For weather claims, the interest rate is tied to the state post-judgment rate plus five percentage points rather than the older flat 18%. The Texas Department of Insurance summarizes the basics on its <a href="https://tdi.texas.gov/blog/insurance-claim-deadlines.html">insurance claim deadlines page</a>.</p>

<h2>Texas HB 1774 and the 61-day notice before suit</h2>
<p>In 2017, Texas passed House Bill 1774, which created Chapter 542A of the Insurance Code. It applies to first-party property claims caused wholly or partly by forces of nature, including hurricanes, wind, hail, floods and tornadoes.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Written notice at least 61 days before suit.</strong> The notice must describe the specific acts or omissions in dispute, the amount you believe the insurer owes and the attorney fees incurred so far.</li>
<li><strong>Inspection right.</strong> The insurer can request to inspect, photograph or evaluate the property within 30 days of receiving the notice.</li>
<li><strong>Abatement.</strong> If notice is not given properly, the insurer can ask the court to pause the case.</li>
<li><strong>Attorney fee limits.</strong> Fees can be reduced or eliminated if the final judgment is far below the amount demanded in the notice.</li>
</ul>
<p>Notice is not required if there is not enough time before the limitations period runs out. That is one more reason not to wait. Before sending a pre-suit notice, talk to a Texas attorney, since the details affect what you can recover.</p>

<h2>The appraisal clause and other ways to resolve disputes</h2>
<p>If you and your insurer agree the damage is covered but disagree on the amount, check your policy for an appraisal clause. Each side typically hires its own appraiser, the two appraisers choose an umpire, and an agreement by any two of them sets the amount of loss. Appraisal is usually faster and cheaper than a lawsuit, but it settles the dollar amount, not whether something is covered.</p>
<p>Other options include asking for a re-inspection with a different adjuster, submitting your own contractor's estimate, filing a complaint with TDI, mediation and hiring a licensed public adjuster. Our guide to <a href="/blog/public-adjuster-vs-insurance-adjuster/">public adjusters vs insurance adjusters</a> explains who works for whom.</p>
<p>Watch the clock on lawsuits, too. Many Texas homeowners policies require suit within two years and one day, and Texas law does not allow a contractual limitations period shorter than two years from when the claim accrues. Read your policy's "suit against us" section and get legal advice well before any deadline.</p>

<h2>Documentation that wins hurricane claims</h2>
<p>After a storm, adjusters handle thousands of claims. The homeowners who get paid fastest make it easy for them.</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Photograph before and after.</strong> Pre-storm photos of your roof, siding and rooms are gold. After the storm, shoot wide and close-up photos and video of every damaged area, including water lines on walls.</li>
<li><strong>Make temporary repairs.</strong> Most policies require reasonable steps to prevent further damage, such as tarping a roof or boarding windows. Keep receipts and photos.</li>
<li><strong>Separate wind and flood evidence.</strong> Note where water came from: above, sideways or rising from the ground.</li>
<li><strong>Keep a claim log.</strong> Record every call, email, visit and promise with names and dates.</li>
<li><strong>Inventory contents.</strong> List each item, its age, cost and replacement cost. Use our <a href="/tools/insurance-claim-depreciation-calculator/">depreciation calculator</a> to check the actual cash value math.</li>
<li><strong>Get your own estimate.</strong> A detailed contractor estimate gives you something concrete to compare against the adjuster's numbers.</li>
</ol>
